A straight line through the origin is direct variation, not inverse. An inverse variation graph is curved and avoids both axes.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}]
\draw[->] (0,0)--(5.3,0) node[right] {$x$};
\draw[->] (0,0)--(0,5.3) node[above] {$y$};
\draw[thick,blue] (0,0)--(4.6,4.6);
\fill (0,0) circle (2.5pt);
\node[below left] at (0,0) {$O$};
\node at (1.6,4.6) {$y=kx$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

A curve that meets either axis is neither direct nor inverse variation, even though it decreases.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}]
\draw[->] (0,0)--(5.3,0) node[right] {$x$};
\draw[->] (0,0)--(0,5.3) node[above] {$y$};
